WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.261
What a loop costs, and how long it takes.

2
00:00:02.541 --> 00:00:05.485
Run the calculator and the loop costs about $2.

3
00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:07.899
14 against about $0.

4
00:00:08.179 --> 00:00:13.853
064 for the single call, roughly 33 times as much, before a single retry.

5
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.779
The structure matters more than the exact numbers.

6
00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:29.475
A stateless API means each step resends the whole history, so total input tokens are a sum of a growing sequence: n times the base plus the growth rate times n(n minus 1)/2.

7
00:00:29.755 --> 00:00:33.083
Doubling the number of steps roughly quadruples the growth term.

8
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:39.080
Prompt caching changes the price of that resent prefix substantially (on Claude Opus 5.

9
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:57.621
5, cache hits are priced at 5% of base input, per Anthropic’s pricing page today), and you should always use it in a loop, but it doesn’t change the fact that each step adds context the model must read, and long contexts carry their own quality and limit problems (Studio 1, lesson 1).

10
00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:11.896
Anthropic’s multi-agent research post gives production figures in the same direction: in their data, agents used about four times as many tokens as chat interactions, and multi-agent systems about fifteen times.

11
00:01:12.176 --> 00:01:17.083
They concluded multi-agent systems need tasks valuable enough to pay for that.

12
00:01:17.363 --> 00:01:18.920
Latency compounds too.

13
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.619
Model calls in a loop are sequential by nature: step 5 needs step 4’s observation.

14
00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:30.211
If each call takes several seconds (more when the model thinks hard), twenty steps take minutes.

15
00:01:30.491 --> 00:01:40.581
That rules out agents for anything a user waits on synchronously, and it shapes the product: progress updates, background jobs, and notifications instead of a spinner.

16
00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:55.261
Kapoor and colleagues make the evaluation version of this point in “AI Agents That Matter” (2024): agent benchmarks that report accuracy alone have rewarded needlessly complex and costly systems.

17
00:01:55.541 --> 00:02:01.344
They argue for evaluating cost and accuracy jointly, and for comparing against simple baselines.

18
00:02:01.624 --> 00:02:11.949
For your team, that means every agent proposal should come with the cost per completed task, not per call, and a comparison with the simplest alternative on the same eval.
